 Do not make anything up in your answer.\n\nText:\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n2746,COPPER,\"Copper, also known as \"\"cobre,\"\" is an essential trace mineral that plays a vital role in various bodily functions. It is necessary for health in low concentrations but can be toxic at high levels. The dietary reference intake for copper varies across different age groups, and specific intake recommendations are outlined in dietary guidelines. \n\nDeficiency of copper is common in certain medical conditions, such as after biliopancreatic diversion and in patients undergoing hematopoietic cell transplantation, which can lead to various health issues. In infants receiving nutritional supplementation, copper levels are closely monitored to prevent toxicity. Excessive copper can lead to serious health conditions, including Wilson's disease, and may contribute to oxidative stress, potentially impacting neurodegenerative diseases.\n\nCopper is also studied for its potential role in various diseases, including cancer. In the context of parenteral nutrition, copper is considered a contaminant, and its levels should be carefully managed to avoid toxicity. Overall, copper is a crucial micronutrient that must be maintained within a balanced range to support health effectively.\",16\r\n2216,ENFERMEDAD DE WILSON,\"Enfermedad de Wilson, also known as Wilson's disease, is a rare genetic disorder related to copper metabolism, first described in humans in 1912. It is caused by mutations in the ATP7B gene, leading to excessive accumulation of copper in the body. This accumulation primarily affects the liver and the central nervous system, but it can also impact other organs such as the kidneys and cornea. The disorder is inherited in an autosomal recessive manner, meaning that both parents must carry the gene mutation for a child to be affected.\n\nIndividuals with Enfermedad de Wilson may experience a range of health complications, including liver disease, neurological symptoms, and systemic effects, with symptoms often appearing in individuals under 40 years of age. Dietary control of copper intake is essential for managing the condition and preventing serious health issues. The disorder is characterized by decreased biliary excretion of copper, which contributes to its harmful buildup in the body. Overall, Enfermedad de Wilson is a significant health concern that requires careful monitoring and management to mitigate its effects.\",41\r\n11828,CUPROPLASMINA,\"Proteína que transporta cobre en la sangre, cuya deficiencia puede estar relacionada con la enfermedad de Wilson.\",2\r\n11815,ATP7B,\"ATP7B is a gene located on chromosome 13 that plays a critical role in copper transport and the elimination of excess copper from the body. It is associated with Wilson's disease, a genetic disorder characterized by the accumulation of copper in various tissues due to mutations in the ATP7B gene. The analysis of ATP7B is essential for the genetic diagnosis of Wilson's disease and for conducting family screening to identify at-risk individuals.\",4\r\n11886,SYPRINE,\"Nombre comercial de trientina, un medicamento utilizado como quelante alternativo en pacientes intolerantes a la penicilamina\",2\r\n11891,ACETATO DE ZINC,Acetato de zinc is a treatment for Wilson's disease that blocks intestinal copper absorption and is used as a chronic maintenance therapy.,1\r\n11890,ASOCIACIÓN ESPAÑOLA DE FAMILIARES Y ENFERMOS DE WILSON,\"The Asociación Española de Familiares y Enfermos de Wilson is an organization that provides support for families and patients affected by Wilson's disease, offering resources and information through their website.\",1\r\n11872,CALCIFICACIONES ARTICULARES,Las calcificaciones articulares son depósitos de calcio en las articulaciones que pueden estar presentes en pacientes con enfermedad de Wilson.,1\r\n11873,CUCURRIA,\"La cupruria es la eliminación de cobre en la orina, que se utiliza para evaluar la presencia de la enfermedad de Wilson.\",1\r\n11874,CUPREMIA,,1\r\n11839,CÓRNEA,\"The cornea is affected in Wilson's disease due to copper deposits, which can lead to vision problems.\",1\r\n11783,MUTACIÓN DEL GEN ATP7B,Mutación del gen ATP7B is a genetic mutation associated with the Enfermedad de Wilson.,1\r\n11840,RIÑONES,\"Riñones, or kidneys, are organ meats known for their high levels of purines, which can contribute to the risk of hyperuricemia. Additionally, the kidneys are affected by Wilson's disease, a condition characterized by the accumulation of copper in the body, leading to potential renal dysfunction.\",1\r\n11869,KAYSER-FLEISCHER RING,\"Anillo de Kayser-Fleischer es un depósito de cobre que se puede observar en los ojos de los pacientes con enfermedad de Wilson, utilizado como un indicador diagnóstico.\",1\r\n11870,POLIARTRITIS,La poliartritis es una inflamación de múltiples articulaciones que puede ser un síntoma asociado a la enfermedad de Wilson.,1\r\n11871,FRACTURAS ESPONTÁNEAS,Las fracturas espontáneas pueden ocurrir en pacientes con enfermedad de Wilson debido a la debilidad ósea.,1\r\n11892,SÍNDROME DE HEMOLÍTICO,Hemolytic syndrome is a condition that can occur as a complication of Wilson's disease due to liver dysfunction and copper toxicity.,1\r\n11893,METALOTIONEÍNAS,\"Metallothioneins are proteins that bind metals, including copper, and play a role in the body's response to copper overload in Wilson's disease.\",1\r\n11894,QUINOLONAS,\"Quinolonas son una clase de antibióticos que pueden estar relacionados con la aparición de diarrea. Además, estos antibióticos pueden interactuar con minerales presentes en la dieta, lo que puede afectar su eficacia. También se ha observado que las quinolonas pueden interferir con tratamientos para la enfermedad de Wilson, especialmente en lo que respecta a la absorción de cobre.\",1\r\n11884,CRIBADO FAMILIAR,\"Cribado familiar refers to the genetic screening of family members for hereditary conditions, particular